-## Critical pitfall: Graph subscriptions expire in 72 hours
+## When to Use
+
+The user is asking to:
+
+- Summarize a Teams meeting / extract action items / pull meeting notes
+- Check pipeline status, inspect a stored meeting job, or see recent meetings
+- Replay / re-run a stored job that failed or needs a fresh summary
+- Validate Microsoft Graph setup after changing env or config
+- Troubleshoot "meeting summary never arrived" or "no new meetings are ingesting"
+- Manage Graph webhook subscriptions (create, renew, delete, inspect)
+- Set up automated subscription renewal
+
+## Not For
+
+- **Scheduling meetings or managing calendar invites** → use `outlook-calendar` or `google-calendar` skill instead
+- **Real-time meeting transcription during a live call** → use `teams-live-transcription` or `whisper` skill instead
+- **General Microsoft 365 administration (users, groups, SharePoint)** → use `microsoft-graph-admin` skill instead
+- **Email-based meeting summaries forwarded from Outlook** → use `email-parsing` or `himalaya` skill instead
+- **Zoom, Google Meet, or Webex meetings** → use the respective platform-specific skill instead
+- **Webhook listener infrastructure setup (reverse proxy, TLS)** → use `nginx-config` or `caddy` skill instead
+
+## Pitfalls
+
+1. **Graph subscriptions expire in 72 hours** — Microsoft Graph caps webhook subscriptions at 72h and **will not auto-renew them**. Run `hermes teams-pipeline maintain-subscriptions` regularly (every 12h via cron). If empty or all expired, recreate with `hermes teams-pipeline subscribe`.
+
+2. **Transcript not available yet** — Teams takes 2-5 minutes after a meeting ends to generate the transcript artifact. `fetch --meeting-id` on a just-ended meeting may return empty. Wait and retry, or let the Graph webhook drive ingestion naturally.
+
+3. **Delivery mode mismatch** — If summaries are produced (`list` shows success) but nothing lands in Teams, check `platforms.teams.extra.delivery_mode` and the matching target config (`incoming_webhook_url` OR `chat_id` OR `team_id`+`channel_id`). The writer reads these from config.yaml or `TEAMS_*` env vars.
+
+4. **Graph app permissions not re-consented** — A token acquires cleanly (`token-health` passes) but Graph API calls return 401/403 when permissions were added but admin consent wasn't re-granted. Revisit the app registration in Azure portal and click "Grant admin consent" again.
+
+5. **Missing env vars** — Verify `MSGRAPH_TENANT_ID`, `MSGRAPH_CLIENT_ID`, `MSGRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET` are set in `~/.hermes/.env`. Use `hermes teams-pipeline validate` to confirm.
+
+6. **Token expired or not refreshing** — Run `hermes teams-pipeline token-health`. If expired, use `--force-refresh` to acquire a fresh token. Check that the client secret hasn't expired in Azure AD.
+
+7. **Subscription notification URL unreachable** — The `--notification-url` must be publicly accessible (not localhost). If behind NAT, use a reverse proxy or tunnel. Verify with `curl https://<your-public-host>/msgraph/webhook`.
+
+8. **Job ID not found in list** — The pipeline is subscription-driven, not per-meeting. If a meeting doesn't appear in `list`, check `subscriptions` first. For past meetings, use `fetch --meeting-id <id>` to pull transcript manually.
 
-Microsoft Graph caps webhook subscriptions at 72 hours and **will not auto-renew them**. If `maintain-subscriptions` is not scheduled, meeting notifications silently stop arriving 3 days after any manual subscription creation.
+9. **Replay fails with same error** — Inspect with `show <job-id>`, then dry-run with `fetch --meeting-id <id>` to isolate whether the issue is transcript resolution or summary generation.
 
